# Environment

## Virtualenv

```shell
uv venv --python 3.10.4 .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
uv pip sync requirements.lock \
    --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 \
    --index-url https://pypi.org/simple \
    --index-strategy unsafe-best-match
```

## Regenerating the lock

The committed `requirements.lock` is built for CUDA 12.8 wheels and Python 3.10.
`--python-version` matters: resolving with a newer interpreter drops the
conditional dependencies that Python 3.10 still needs, such as `async-timeout`,
`exceptiongroup`, and `tomli`.

```shell
uv pip compile requirements.in \
    -o requirements.lock \
    --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 \
    --index-url https://pypi.org/simple \
    --override overrides.txt \
    --index-strategy unsafe-best-match \
    --python-version 3.10.4
```

The `cu128` wheels bundle their own CUDA runtime, so the driver is what has to be
new enough, not the system toolkit: they run on any driver from the CUDA 12.x
family. Swap the index URL for another variant (for example `cu126`) only if a
driver turns out to be too old.

## Java for PIRB

PIRB retrieval runs on Anserini through pyserini, and pyserini 0.39 ships a fat
jar built for Java 21. A system JDK is often older, which fails at JVM startup
with a `jnius.JavaException` while loading the Anserini classes.

`third_party/pirb/utils/system.py` reads `JAVA_HOME` and otherwise guesses the
JDK from `which java`, so an older default JDK is picked up silently. Export the
right one before running a benchmark or the full test suite:

```shell
export JAVA_HOME=/path/to/jdk-21.0.5+11
```

If the machine has no JDK 21, install one next to the project without touching
the system default:

```shell
mkdir -p ~/opt && cd ~/opt
curl -fsSL -o jdk21.tar.gz \
    "https://github.com/adoptium/temurin21-binaries/releases/download/jdk-21.0.5%2B11/OpenJDK21U-jdk_x64_linux_hotspot_21.0.5_11.tar.gz"
tar xzf jdk21.tar.gz && rm jdk21.tar.gz
export JAVA_HOME=$HOME/opt/jdk-21.0.5+11
```

`scripts/pirb_resume_e2e.py` downloads the same build into its own `--root`, so
it stays independent of this setup.

## Tests

```shell
pytest -q
```

`pytest` is part of `requirements.lock`. Tests under `third_party/pirb/` start a
real JVM, so they need `JAVA_HOME` from the section above; everything under
`tests/` runs without it.
